Name F_OWNER_TID only where the system has it.

The fcntl module adds the struct f_owner_ex constants when F_GETOWN_EX is
defined and takes F_OWNER_TID to come with it, as on Linux.  POSIX.1-2024
has F_GETOWN_EX, F_SETOWN_EX, F_OWNER_PID and F_OWNER_PGRP but no
F_OWNER_TID, which zedBSD's <fcntl.h> follows (a signal's owner is a
process or a process group, not a thread), so the module asks for that one
on its own.

--- a/Modules/fcntlmodule.c
+++ b/Modules/fcntlmodule.c
@@ -614,7 +614,9 @@
     // since Linux 2.6.32
     if (PyModule_AddIntMacro(m, F_GETOWN_EX)) return -1;
     if (PyModule_AddIntMacro(m, F_SETOWN_EX)) return -1;
+#ifdef F_OWNER_TID
     if (PyModule_AddIntMacro(m, F_OWNER_TID)) return -1;
+#endif
     if (PyModule_AddIntMacro(m, F_OWNER_PID)) return -1;
     if (PyModule_AddIntMacro(m, F_OWNER_PGRP)) return -1;
 #endif
